details.image_prompt:
[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene features two ceramic bowls containing the warm berry and almond skillet crisp, centered in the frame with generous negative space to accommodate a circular crop. Captured from a 3/4 overhead angle (40°), ensuring every component—the smooth white yogurt base, the deep purple-red cooked berry compote, and the golden, toasted oat-almond topping—is clearly visible and identifiable. The foreground bowl is in sharp focus, while the second bowl sits slightly behind and to the side, softly blurred. Lighting is soft, natural, and diffused from a window on the left, casting subtle, gentle shadows and highlighting the sticky sheen of the maple-glazed berries. The bowls rest on a weathered, rustic oak wood surface with visible grain. Props include a small wooden spoon resting casually beside the front bowl, a few stray sliced almonds and a pinch of cinnamon scattered naturally on the table, and a small glass jar of maple syrup slightly out of focus in the background. The berries appear soft, partially mashed, and glossy, with a rich, dark berry-red hue. The topping is golden-brown with varied textures of toasted oats and almond slivers, showing slight color variations from the heat. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric sprinkle of the topping, a small drop of berry juice on the rim of the bowl, slightly uneven browning on the oats. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a few stray crumbs on the wooden surface and a dusting of cinnamon powder. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting, not cold or clinical. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
]
